Comment (main.go:78):
`Eval` takes a full write `Lock` on the `RWMutex`, but only the lazy-init branch (parsedConfig/policyData) needs the write lock. The common path is read-only and could use `RLock`.
Replies:
**lisa implementation guidance:** Refactor `CompliancePlugin.Eval` in `main.go` so the common already-configured path uses `RLock`. First take `l.mu.RLock()`, read whether `parsedConfig` and `policyData` are both non-nil, and if so copy the needed values while still protected: assign `parsedConfig := l.parsedConfig`, `policyData := clonePolicyInputs(l.policyData)`, and `policyLabels := cloneStringMap(parsedConfig.PolicyLabels)`, then `RUnlock`. If either field is nil, release the read lock, take `Lock`, lazily initialize `parsedConfig` and/or `policyData` exactly as today, copy the same local values under the write lock, then unlock. Be careful not to read `l.policyData` or `l.parsedConfig` after unlocking, and keep error handling for `parsePluginConfig` unchanged.
